% === provenance: RashkovitsLavy2011 ===
% CLAIM: Novice programmers who do use the exception mechanism commonly
%   use only the single general exception class rather than distinguishing
%   kinds of error.
% FOUND-VIA: scholar search, session exceptions-novice, topic-driven:
%   'TITLE-ABS-KEY("exception handling" AND programming AND (novice OR
%   students OR learners) AND (misconception OR "mental model" OR
%   comprehension))' -p scopus; the same concept query was run on
%   openalex, dblp, ieee and wos (TS=(...)).
% PICKED: a primary empirical study of exactly the population the deck
%   addresses (students in an introductory/undergraduate setting) that
%   reports how they *used* the mechanism, not merely whether they liked
%   it; open access, so the full text could be read.  Preferred over the
%   authors' 2012 companion for this particular claim because the 2012
%   study measures comprehension of advanced properties, whereas this one
%   measures what the students actually wrote.
% QUOTE (p. 183, Executive Summary): "The results obtained reveal that
%   only few participants (7 out of 56) provided a solution that was
%   classified to one of the two highest assimilation levels, while many
%   (23 out of 56) did not use exception mechanism at all. The rest of the
%   students (25 out of 56) used the exception mechanism poorly (i.e.,
%   used only Exception class or did not use hierarchy of exceptions)."
% QUOTE (p. 203): "In many cases, even when the students decided to use
%   the exception handling mechanism, they did not derive exceptions from
%   java.lang.Exception as they should have done. Instead, they used the
%   java.lang.Exception itself and used the message attribute to
%   differentiate one exception from another."
% VERIFIED: full text read (PDF from jite.org, the journal's own open
%   archive, 2026-09-03).  Scope caveat recorded in bilaga B: the study
%   is on Java and on students at the end of their degree, so it is
%   evidence that the general-class habit is real and survives teaching,
%   not that it holds at the same rate for first-year Python students.
% COUNTER: same session; 'TS=("exception handling" AND (novice OR students
%   OR learners) AND (teaching OR education OR curriculum) AND (effective
%   OR successful OR "no difficulty" OR critique OR limitations))' and
%   'TS=("exception handling" AND (students OR novices OR learners) AND
%   ("flow of control" OR "control flow" OR propagation) AND (understand
%   OR mastery OR "not a difficulty" OR overstated))' on openalex, dblp,
%   ieee, wos and scopus.  No study arguing that students do not have
%   these difficulties was returned; nothing to refute or qualify beyond
%   the scope caveat above.
% DATE: 2026-09-03
@article{RashkovitsLavy2011,
  author  = {Rashkovits, Rami and Lavy, Ilana},
  title   = {Students' Strategies for Exception Handling},
  journal = {Journal of Information Technology Education: Research},
  volume  = {10},
  pages   = {183--207},
  year    = {2011},
  doi     = {10.28945/1500}
}

